{
  "image_name": "04d38d2e393ab353c0c58731c16296d2.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the main design features of concentric, shell-like particles by grouping them into four broad categories: crystal properties, pore structure, surface properties, and higher-order assembly. On the crystal side, it shows that these particles can be built from inorganic, organic, or hybrid components, and that their shells may range from single-crystal to polycrystalline or amorphous, with different geometric orientations and shapes.\n\nA second theme is pore architecture. The image highlights that pores can vary in size from micro- to meso- to macroporous, can be distributed differently across the shell, and can be aligned at various angles. It also contrasts more ordered pore arrangements with disordered ones.\n\nSurface properties are presented as tunable features of the shell exterior and interior, including chemical decoration, wettability, and roughness. The sketches suggest that molecules or functional groups can be attached to surfaces, changing how liquids or other species interact with the particle.\n\nFinally, the figure emphasizes assembly across scales. Individual shell subunits appear to be organized through organic groups and then packed into larger superstructures, including rings, tubes, layered arrangements, and particles attached onto bigger supports. Overall, the image conveys a modular framework for engineering these complex hollow materials.",
